SUPER KEYWORD — SHORT NOTES

1. super refers to the immediate parent class.

2. super() is used to call the parent class constructor.

3. super() must be the FIRST statement inside the child constructor.

4. If we don't write super(), Java automatically adds:
   super();

5. If we write super("value"), it calls the matching
   parameterized constructor of the parent class.

6. If super("value") is explicitly written, the parent
   default constructor is NOT called.

7. Only ONE super() constructor can be called from a
   child constructor.

8. super.variable → Access parent class variable.

9. super.method() → Call parent class method.

Example:

class Vehicle {
    Vehicle() {
        System.out.println("Vehicle");
    }

    Vehicle(String model) {
        System.out.println("Vehicle Model");
    }
}

class Car extends Vehicle {
    Car() {
        super("BMW");   // Calls Vehicle(String)
        System.out.println("Car");
    }
}

Output:
Vehicle Model
Car


IMPORTANT:
super()              → Parent default constructor
super(value)         → Parent parameterized constructor
super.variable       → Parent variable
super.method()       → Parent method

===============
super.variable → Parent class variable

Can be used inside:
    ✅ Child method
    ✅ Child constructor
    ✅ Child instance block

    =======================

    super.method() → Calls the parent class method.

    It can be used inside the child class:
        ✅ Child method
        ✅ Child constructor
        ✅ Child instance block